WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.200
The Tasmanian devil is a carnivorous
marsupial, in fact it is the largest

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.029
living carnivorous marsupial in the world.
It is the top predator of Tasmania

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:11.380
and as a specialized scavenger also has
a recycling job

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.440
which is to clear, all day, dead
animals that are in the landscape.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.000
So it has a very important ecological role
in keeping the ecosystem in a good balance.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.800
Tasmanian devils are solitary, strictly
speaking they don't live

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.020
in groups or in family groups but
despite that they are quite social.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:31.480
Quite social, but they're not that friendly
to each other so they can be

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.600
quite aggressive when having disputes
over meat for example, over food

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.800
so growling and biting is part of a normal Tasmanian devil's life.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:46.800
The Tasmanian devils are affected by a very horrible disease called Devil Facial Tumour Disease

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:51.510
and this disease is characterised by large,
cancerous lesions that form around the

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.800
face and the neck of the affected devils. 

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.200
The disease kills the
devils very quickly

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.690
and it's thought to have a hundred
percent mortality so it's had a

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.300
devastating effect on the population.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.229
Devil Facial Tumour Disease

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:09.409
is a very strange and bizarre new type
of cancer

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:12.650
which has arisen recently in the Tasmanian devil population.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:17.300
It's actually a cancer which is
transmissible, so it can be spread between devils.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.580
These tumours were first seen by

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.000
a wildlife photographer in the far North East of Tasmania in 1996.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.979
The disease turned up at my
long-term study site

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:33.720
on the Freycinet Peninsula on the East Coast of Tasmania in 2001.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.700
Within 12 months of seeing the disease at my study site

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:44.720
I realised that it was both spreading and it was causing major population decline.

